---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/wire-size';
import { GAUGES, gaugeLabel, usableAmps, sizeWire, type Gauge } from '../lib/electrical';

const table = GAUGES.slice(0, 13).map((g) => ({
  g,
  cu60: usableAmps('cu', '60', g as Gauge),
  cu75: usableAmps('cu', '75', g as Gauge),
  al60: usableAmps('al', '60', g as Gauge),
  al75: usableAmps('al', '75', g as Gauge),
}));
// 240 V、单相、3% 电压降、60°C：不同负载和距离下需要的铜线线径
const loads = [15, 20, 30, 40, 50];
const dists = [25, 50, 100, 150, 200];
// 12 V 直流（房车、太阳能）：3% 电压降下的铜线线径
const dcLoads = [5, 10, 20, 30];
const dcDists = [5, 10, 15, 20, 30];
const pickDc = (a: number, d: number) => sizeWire({ metal: 'cu', amps: a, oneWayFt: d, volts: 12, phase: 'dc', maxDropPct: 3, column: '60', continuous: false }).pick;
const pick = (a: number, d: number) => sizeWire({ metal: 'cu', amps: a, oneWayFt: d, volts: 240, phase: '1', maxDropPct: 3, column: '60', continuous: false }).pick;
---

<CalcPage
  slug="wire-size-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Wire Size Calculator: AWG by Amps, Distance & Voltage Drop"
  description="Free wire size calculator. Enter amps, distance, and voltage to find the minimum copper or aluminum AWG wire that meets NEC ampacity and a 3% voltage drop."
  h1="Wire Size Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Find the smallest copper or aluminum wire that carries your load safely and keeps voltage drop in check over the run,
    using NEC ampacity tables.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how">How wire size is chosen</h2>
  <p>A wire has to pass two separate tests, and the larger of the two answers wins:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Ampacity:</strong> the wire must carry the current without overheating. This comes from NEC Table 310.16,
      limited for small wires by NEC 240.4(D): 14 AWG copper to 15 A, 12 AWG to 20 A, 10 AWG to 30 A.</li>
    <li><strong>Voltage drop:</strong> over a long run, wire resistance wastes voltage. The NEC recommends no more than 3%
      on a branch circuit.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    For short runs, ampacity decides. For long runs, voltage drop usually does: a 20 A, 120 V circuit that needs only 12 AWG
    at the panel needs 8 AWG to stay under 3% at 100 feet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ampacity">Wire ampacity chart</h2>
  <p>NEC Table 310.16, 30°C ambient, up to three current-carrying conductors, with the 240.4(D) small-conductor limits applied.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Size</th><th class="num">Copper 60°C</th><th class="num">Copper 75°C</th><th class="num">Aluminum 60°C</th><th class="num">Aluminum 75°C</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {table.map((r) => <tr><td>{gaugeLabel(r.g)}</td><td class="num">{r.cu60 ?? '—'} A</td><td class="num">{r.cu75 ?? '—'} A</td><td class="num">{r.al60 !== undefined ? `${r.al60} A` : '—'}</td><td class="num">{r.al75 !== undefined ? `${r.al75} A` : '—'}</td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="distance">Copper wire size by amps and distance at 240 V</h2>
  <p>Single phase, 3% maximum voltage drop, 60°C terminals. Distance is one way.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Load</th>{dists.map((d) => <th class="num">{d} ft</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{loads.map((a) => <tr><td>{a} A</td>{dists.map((d) => { const g = pick(a, d); return <td class="num">{g ? gaugeLabel(g) : '—'}</td>; })}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="dc">Wire size for 12 V DC: solar, RV, and boat circuits</h2>
  <p>
    At 12 volts, 3% is only 0.36 V, so voltage drop decides the wire size almost every time. Choose <em>DC</em> in the
    calculator and pick 12, 24, or 48 V. Doubling the system voltage halves the current for the same power, which is why
    larger solar systems run at 24 or 48 V.
  </p>
  <p>Copper, 12 V DC, 3% maximum voltage drop. Distance is one way.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Load</th>{dcDists.map((d) => <th class="num">{d} ft</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{dcLoads.map((a) => <tr><td>{a} A</td>{dcDists.map((d) => { const g = pickDc(a, d); return <td class="num">{g ? gaugeLabel(g) : '—'}</td>; })}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Wiring a new mini-split or window unit? <a href="/btu-calculator/">Size the unit in BTUs</a> first; its nameplate then gives the current
    to enter here.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Should I use the 60°C or 75°C column?</h3>
  <p>
    Under NEC 110.14(C), circuits of 100 A or less use the 60°C column unless every terminal, breaker, and device is marked
    for 75°C. Many modern breakers are, but the safe default for household branch circuits is 60°C.
  </p>
  <h3>What counts as a continuous load?</h3>
  <p>
    A load expected to run at full current for three hours or more, like EV chargers, water heaters, and some heaters.
    Conductors for continuous loads are sized at 125% of the current.
  </p>
  <h3>What doesn't this calculator cover?</h3>
  <p>
    Derating for more than three current-carrying conductors together, high ambient temperatures (attics and rooftops),
    motor circuits, and service conductors. Those change the answer, so have an electrician confirm anything beyond a
    simple circuit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Checking an existing run? Use the <a href="/voltage-drop-calculator/">voltage drop calculator</a>. Pulling several
    wires through a pipe? See the <a href="/conduit-fill-calculator/">conduit fill calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ampacity from NEC Table 310.16, capped by 240.4(D) for 14–10 AWG; continuous loads are sized at 125%.</li>
    <li>Voltage drop = 2 × length × current × resistance ÷ 1,000 (single phase) or √3 × length × current × resistance ÷ 1,000 (three phase), using NEC Chapter 9 Table 8 resistance at 75°C.</li>
    <li>The result is the smallest size, 14 AWG to 500 kcmil, that passes both tests. This is a planning tool, not a substitute for your local code or a licensed electrician.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
